      "text": "To find the largest value of $c$ such that $-2$ is in the range of the function $f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c$, we need to set $f(x) = -2$ and solve for $x$.\n\nFrom $f(x) = -2$, we have:\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\]\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0 \\]\n\nFor $-2$ to be in the range of $f(x)$, this quadratic equation must have at least one real solution. This means the discriminant of the quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant $\\Delta$ of the quadratic equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ is given by $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$. For our equation $x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0$, the coefficients are $a = 1$, $b = 3$, and $c = c + 2$. So the discriminant is:\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4(1)(c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2) = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c \\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have at least one real solution, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0 \\]\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of $c$ such that $-2$ is in the range of $f(x)$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}$.",
