      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to find \\( c \\) such that there exists some \\( x \\) for which \\( f(x) = -2 \\).\n\nStarting with the equation:\n\\[ f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\]\nwe can rearrange it to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0 \\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0. \\]\n\nFor \\(-2\\) to be in the range of \\( f(x) \\), the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\) must have at least one real solution. A quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) has real solutions if and only if its discriminant is non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\) is given by:\n\\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac, \\]\nwhere \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2) = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c. \\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real solutions, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0. \\]\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\), we get:\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c, \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4}. \\]\n\nThe largest value of \\( c \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( c = \\frac{1}{4} \\). Therefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}. \\]",
