      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to set \\( f(x) = -2 \\) and solve for \\( c \\). Here are the steps:\n\n1. Set \\( f(x) = -2 \\):\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 3x + c = -2\n   \\]\n   Rearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n   \\]\n   Let \\( g(x) = x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) \\). For \\(-2\\) to be in the range of \\( f(x) \\), \\( g(x) \\) must have at least one real root. This means the quadratic equation \\( g(x) = 0 \\) must have real solutions.\n\n2. For the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\) to have real solutions, its discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n   \\]\n   Here, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). So, the discriminant is:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2) = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c\n   \\]\n   For the quadratic equation to have real solutions, the discriminant must be greater than or equal to zero:\n   \\[\n   1 - 4c \\geq 0\n   \\]\n   Solving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n   \\[\n   1 \\geq 4c \\implies \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c \\implies c \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. Therefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n   \\]",
