      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to determine when the equation \\( f(x) = -2 \\) has a real solution. This means we need to solve the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to standard quadratic form:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0\n\\]\n\nFor this quadratic equation to have real solutions, its discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our equation \\(x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0\\), the coefficients are \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 3\\), and \\(c = c + 2\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the discriminant:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real solutions, the discriminant must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\geq c\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThe largest value of \\( c \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( c = \\frac{1}{4} \\). Therefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
